1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
In a small town in Japan during the 1920s, there was a professor named Haidis Aburo Wano,

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
who taught at Tokyo University. Every morning, he would walk to the nearby Shibuya train station

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
with his loyal dog, Hachiko, a golden brown akeeta. After saying goodbye, Hachiko would wait

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
patiently at the station for his master to return each evening. One day, however, tragedy struck.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Professor Wano suffered a sudden brain hemorrhage while at work and passed away.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
He never returned to the station. But Hachiko didn't know that. Every day, for the next nine years,

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Hachiko continued to come to Shibuya station at the same time, waiting for his beloved master.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Rain or shine through the harsh winters and hot summers, he waited, sitting quietly by the

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
entrance, his eyes searching every face in the crowd. At first, the people at the station were

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
indifferent. But over time, they noticed the dog's unwavering loyalty. Passengers and station

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
workers began to bring him food and treats. His story spread across Japan, touching the hearts

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,000
of thousands, and symbolizing the pure loyalty and devotion that animals and people are capable of.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
After Hachiko's death in 1935, a bronze statue was erected in his honor at Shibuya station.

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
To this day, the statue stands as a beloved meeting spot, and a reminder that true loyalty

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
never fades, even when hope is gone.

